### Summary
A heap buffer overflow (out-of-bounds write and out-of-bounds read) vulnerability exists in the progressive JPEG decoder (`jpeg_decode_progressive_scan`) within `third_party/iris/jpeg.h`. This vulnerability can be triggered remotely and without authentication via the `POST /v1/chat/completions` endpoint when the server is run with the `--vision` flag.

---

### Root Cause Analysis
In `third_party/iris/jpeg.h`, the spectral parameters `Ss` and `Se` from the SOS marker are copied directly from the incoming file structure without prior validation:

```c
/* jpeg.h */
dec.ss = file_data[sos_offset];
dec.se = file_data[sos_offset + 1];
```

These parameters are subsequently used as loop bounds to index the 64-element `jpeg_zigzag` lookup table:

```c
/* jpeg.h */
int k = dec->ss;
while (k <= dec->se) {
    ...
    coef[jpeg_zigzag[k]] = (int16_t)(ac_val * (1 << dec->al));
```

If a malformed JPEG specifies an `Se` value greater than 63 (up to 255), `k` goes out of bounds of the `jpeg_zigzag` array. The arbitrary byte read from memory is then used as a write index into the `coef` buffer. Since `coef` is allocated dynamically based on blocks (e.g., a minimal 1x1 image allocates only 128 bytes), this leads to a severe heap-buffer-overflow write.

---

### Reachability Chain
1. `POST /v1/chat/completions` (or `/v1/responses`) with a base64-encoded `image/jpeg` payload.
2. `server_image_inputs_push_base64` (`ds4_server.c`) decodes the payload.
3. `server_encode_image` -> `ds4_engine_vision_encode_memory` (`ds4.c`).
4. `ds4_image_decode_memory` (`ds4_image.c`) -> `jpeg_load_mem` -> Trigger.

---

### Proof of Concept (PoC) / Verification

[jpeg_oob_poc.c](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/34011574/jpeg_oob_poc.c)

### Suggested Fix

In `third_party/iris/jpeg.h`, locate the SOS marker parsing logic (around line 1394) and validate the spectral selection parameters immediately after they are read:

```c
dec.ss = file_data[sos_offset];
dec.se = file_data[sos_offset + 1];

// Security Fix: Validate JPEG spectral selection bounds
if (dec.ss > 63 || dec.se > 63 || dec.ss > dec.se) {
    goto fail; 
}
```

Additionally, add a defensive guard inside the progressive decode loop (around line 858) before accessing `jpeg_zigzag`:

```c
int k = dec->ss;
while (k <= dec->se) {
    if (k >= 64) {
        break; // Prevent out-of-bounds read/write
    }
    ...
```
